= i
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Write $B_j^n(t)=\binom njt^j(1-t)^{n-j}$ for the <Bernstein basis>, and set $B_j^n=0$ when $j$ is outside its index range. Differentiating and using $j\binom nj=n\binom{n-1}{j-1}$ and $(n-j)\binom nj=n\binom{n-1}j$ gives
$$
\frac{d}{dt}B_j^n(t)=nB_{j-1}^{n-1}(t)-nB_j^{n-1}(t).
$$
Hence, after shifting the first sum by one index,
$$
\begin{aligned}
P'(t)&=n\sum_{j=0}^nP_jB_{j-1}^{n-1}(t)-n\sum_{j=0}^nP_jB_j^{n-1}(t)\\
&=n\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}(P_{j+1}-P_j)B_j^{n-1}(t).
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus **the <derivative> is a degree-at-most-$(n-1)$ <Bézier curve> whose <control points> are $n(P_{j+1}-P_j)$**. They are vectors rather than necessarily positions on the original <Bézier curve>. This is the <Bézier derivative control polygon>.

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Applying the preceding <derivative> identity repeatedly yields the <Bézier derivative control polygon> for every order:
$$
\boxed{P^{(r)}(t)=\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}\sum_{j=0}^{n-r}\Delta^rP_jB_j^{n-r}(t),\qquad0\le r\le n,}
$$
where the forward <finite difference> is $\Delta P_j=P_{j+1}-P_j$ and
$$
\Delta^rP_j=\sum_{k=0}^r(-1)^{r-k}\binom rkP_{j+k}.
$$
The induction step multiplies by the remaining degree $n-r$ and takes one more <finite difference>. <Derivatives> above order $n$ vanish. In particular,
$$
P^{(r)}(0)=\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}\Delta^rP_0,\qquad P^{(r)}(1)=\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}\Delta^rP_{n-r}.
$$
If the actual parameter interval has length $h$ and $t$ is its normalized coordinate, <derivatives> in the original parameter acquire the additional factor $h^{-r}$. These formulas allow <derivative> evaluation and join tests using only the <control polygon>.

= iii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Let the first <Bézier curve> have degree $n$ and <control points> $P_0,\ldots,P_n$, and the next have degree $m$ and <control points> $Q_0,\ldots,Q_m$. Use one increasing parameter on the two adjacent intervals. For a continuous join, their endpoints must agree: $P_n=Q_0$. If each interval has length $h$, the endpoint <derivatives> are $n(P_n-P_{n-1})/h$ and $m(Q_1-Q_0)/h$. Therefore the <parametric first-derivative join of Bézier curves> is exactly
$$
\boxed{P_n=Q_0,\qquad n(P_n-P_{n-1})=m(Q_1-Q_0).}
$$
For equal degrees, the two polygon edges incident on the common endpoint are equal vectors. Equivalently, $P_{n-1},P_n=Q_0,Q_1$ are collinear with the join as their midpoint. Agreement of edge directions without these lengths gives only geometric tangent <continuity> at a nonzero tangent, not equality of <derivatives> in the chosen parameter. The displayed condition also covers zero endpoint <derivatives>. With unequal interval lengths $h,k$, the condition is instead $n(P_n-P_{n-1})/h=m(Q_1-Q_0)/k$.

= iv
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

A cubic <polynomial> on either half is determined by its endpoint positions and endpoint <derivatives>. Use normalized parameters $s=2t$ on the first half and $s=2t-1$ on the second, so that <derivatives> with respect to $s$ are half the <derivatives> with respect to $t$. Evaluation of the <Bernstein basis> at the split gives
$$
H=P(1/2)=\frac{P_0+3P_1+3P_2+P_3}{8},\qquad P'(1/2)=\frac34(-P_0-P_1+P_2+P_3).
$$
If the left <control points> are $L_0,\ldots,L_3$, endpoint positions give $L_0=P_0,L_3=H$. The cubic endpoint-derivative formulas give $3(L_1-L_0)=P'(0)/2$ and $3(L_3-L_2)=P'(1/2)/2$. On the right, $R_0=H,R_3=P_3$, $3(R_1-R_0)=P'(1/2)/2$ and $3(R_3-R_2)=P'(1)/2$. Substitution yields
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
(L_0,L_1,L_2,L_3)&=\left(P_0,\frac{P_0+P_1}{2},\frac{P_0+2P_1+P_2}{4},H\right),\\
(R_0,R_1,R_2,R_3)&=\left(H,\frac{P_1+2P_2+P_3}{4},\frac{P_2+P_3}{2},P_3\right).
\end{aligned}}
$$
These are exact restricted <Bézier curves>, since they match all four endpoint Hermite data of the respective cubics.

The construction is also the midpoint case of <De Casteljau's algorithm>. Form $A_j=(P_j+P_{j+1})/2$ for $j=0,1,2$, then $B_0=(A_0+A_1)/2$, $B_1=(A_1+A_2)/2$, and $H=(B_0+B_1)/2$. The two polygons are $(P_0,A_0,B_0,H)$ and $(H,B_1,A_2,P_3)$. Their common tangent edges satisfy $H-B_0=B_1-H$, as required for equal half-intervals. Thus the triangular midpoint construction simultaneously evaluates the split point and supplies the two exact representations.

